  "final_text": "Both objects exist: the dashboard has 4 panels and the alert rule is enabled.\n\n**Data exploration:** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` view has about 14,000 requests from roughly 1,000 client IPs across 4 hosts, spread over the last ~10 days. Key fields are `response.keyword` (status codes), `bytes`, `clientip`, `extension`, `machine.os.keyword`, and `@timestamp`. The status codes are 200 (12,832), 404 (801), and 503 (441). The only 5xx code present is 503.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-1687480f Web logs overview\" (`eff-1687480f-web-logs-overview-bb0790c09254`), default range last 30 days.\n- Total requests (metric, count)\n- Average response size in bytes (metric, average of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram on `@timestamp`)\n- Requests by response code (bar, terms on `response.keyword`)\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-1687480f 5xx spike\" (`dd0f63db-08b9-4658-94a5-5384f063805d`), enabled.\n- Type `.es-query` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, checked every 1m.\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5*` in the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Consumer `stackAlerts`, no connector attached, so it will record alerts but won't notify anyone.\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- Used a wildcard `5*` rather than a hardcoded `503`, so the rule still catches other 5xx codes.\n- Left the rule without an action connector, since none was requested.\n\nCurrent status is `pending`. The sample data is older than the 5-minute window, so the rule will likely not fire unless 5xx traffic arrives. I didn't confirm a firing run.",
